On the primary database, issue the following SQL statements to create a global temporary table named gtt and a session sequence named s : SQL> CREATE GLOBAL TEMPORARY TABLE gtt (a int);

Table created.

SQL> CREATE SEQUENCE s SESSION;

Sequence created. On the standby database, issue the following SQL statements: SQL> INSERT INTO gtt VALUES (s.NEXTVAL);

1 row created.

SQL> INSERT INTO gtt VALUES (s.NEXTVAL);

1 row created.


SQL> SELECT * FROM gtt;

         A
----------
         1
         2 From another session of the same standby database, issue the following SQL statements: SQL> INSERT INTO gtt VALUES (s.NEXTVAL);

1 row created.

SQL> INSERT INTO gtt VALUES (s.NEXTVAL);

1 row created.


SQL> SELECT * FROM gtt;

         A
----------
         1
         2 The results of the SELECT statement show that the sequence values assigned are the same as those assigned for the first session in the previous step. This is because sequence values are unique to each database session.

